    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">ME/CFS, moderate, improving with energy management (G93.32)</content>. Fewer crashes and fewer orthostatic symptoms. PROMIS fatigue has fallen from 71 to 67 and physical function has risen from 32 to 35; pain score 3. Both crashes followed days above her baseline. [S]<list><item>Agreed to add 30 minutes of work a day for two weeks and keep it only if the diary shows no post-exertional malaise; she will step back down if it does.</item><item>Continue fluids, salt, and compression.</item><item>Continue the flare plan; no graded exercise.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Vitamin D deficiency (E55.9)</content>. On cholecalciferol for eleven weeks. The planned recheck was not drawn today. [S]<list><item>Continue cholecalciferol 2,000 units daily.</item><item>Draw 25-hydroxyvitamin D at the six-month review.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9 mood screen</td><td>At diagnosis and at each review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>6, down from 8, with item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PROMIS fatigue and physical function T-scores, pain score</td><td>At diagnosis and at each review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Fatigue 67, physical function 35, pain 3</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>25-hydroxyvitamin D about three months after starting repletion</td><td>Once, about three months after starting</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Left off today&apos;s order in error, a deviation from the plan. She is taking the supplement daily and has no symptoms of excess, so no harm is expected; it will be drawn at the six-month review</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Symptom review: post-exertional malaise, sleep, pain, cognition, and orthostatic symptoms</td><td>Every review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two crashes in two months; orthostatic symptoms two or three times a week</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Ten-minute standing test</td><td>Repeat if symptoms change, or at six months</td><td>Due</td><td>Planned at the six-month review</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Energy management plan review</td><td>Every review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Small trial increase of 30 minutes of work a day, to be kept only if the diary shows no post-exertional malaise</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medication review</td><td>Every review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Cholecalciferol daily; acetaminophen about once a week</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Agree an energy management plan with a stable baseline and no more than one crash a month by the six-month review. In progress; target 1858-09-14 [S]</item><item>Keep working within her energy limits, aiming for four hours a day in planned blocks by the six-month review. In progress; target 1858-09-14 [S]</item><item>Fewer orthostatic symptoms with fluids, salt, compression, and seated or reclined tasks by the three-month review. Outcome by 1858-06-01: Lightheaded on standing two or three times a week instead of daily [S]</item><item>Replete vitamin D to 30 ng/mL or higher. In progress; target 1858-09-14 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Myalgic encephalomyelitis (or encephalopathy)/chronic fatigue syndrome: diagnosis and management (NICE guideline NG206) (2021); Diagnosing ME/CFS (2024); Grach SL, et al. Diagnosis and Management of Myalgic Encephalomyelitis/Chronic Fatigue Syndrome. Mayo Clin Proc 2023;98(10):1544-1551 (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: ME/CFS review in about three months with 25-hydroxyvitamin D, PROMIS scores, PHQ-9, GAD-7, and a repeat standing test. Occupational therapist as needed. Next scheduled visit 1858-09-14: six-month ME/CFS care plan review. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
